-- title:
--   Two-chart degree bound on minimal polynomial coefficients
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field and $F$ a field equipped with an $L$-algebra structure, and let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $L$ with $x \neq 0$; write $A = L[x] =$ `Algebra.adjoin L {x}` for the $L$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by $x$. Let $f \in F$ be integral over $A$, and let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ be such that for every valuation subring $V$ of $F$ which contains the image of $L$ under the structure map $L \to F$ and which does not contain $x$, one has $f \cdot (x^m)^{-1} \in V$. Let $j \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $c \in L[X]$ be a polynomial whose value at $x$ equals the $j$-th coefficient of the minimal polynomial of $f$ over $A$, that coefficient being an element of $A$ regarded as an element of $F$. Then the degree of $c$ satisfies $\deg c \le (d - j)\cdot m$, where $d$ is the degree of the minimal polynomial of $f$ over $A$ and the subtraction $d - j$ is truncated subtraction of natural numbers.
--
--   This is the pole-order, or two-chart, degree bound of Deuring's reduction theory for algebraic function fields: integrality of $f$ over $L[x]$ together with the bound $\operatorname{ord}(f) \ge -m$ at all places where $x$ has a pole forces the $j$-th coefficient of the minimal polynomial of $f$ to have $x$-degree at most $(d-j)m$. It is used to produce monic equations with controlled coefficient degrees, feeding the construction of regular prolongations of curves and of fibre models of modular curves on cusp charts.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Polynomial_natDegree_aeval_symm_minpoly_adjoin_coeff_le_of_transcendental.lean

